outputs.exact_statement:
Put $V^a=\underline{\mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}}_{A}(X,FX[a])$ for each integer $a$. There is a unique $v\in V^0$ whose two projections are $\mathop{\mathrm{id}}_X$. It spans $V^0$. For every integer $a>0$, projection induces an isomorphism $$\label{branch:positive-projection}
  (\rho_1[a],\rho_2[a]):V^a\xrightarrow{\sim}
  (H^a)^2,
  \qquad H^a=\underline{\mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}}_{A}(X,X[a]).$$

The finite bimodule $F$ in <a href="#branch:finite-fiber" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="branch:finite-fiber">[branch:finite-fiber]</a> is projective on both $A$-sides, and the map $v$ of Lemma <a href="#branch:profile" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="branch:profile">17</a> satisfies the following conditions. For every integer $a\ge0$, define $$\label{branch:delta}
  \delta^a:H^a\oplus H^a\longrightarrow V^a,
  \qquad (g,j)\longmapsto F(g)v+v[a]j.$$ Then $\delta^0$ is surjective with a one-dimensional kernel, and $\delta^a$ is an isomorphism for every integer $a>0$.
